# Keep the code sharp

The phone app has four layers. A layer only talks to the one below it.

| Folder | Owns | Must not |
|--------|------|----------|
| `lib/screens/` | What the user sees. `ref.watch` in `build`, `ref.read` in callbacks. | HTTP, SharedPreferences, or JSON parsing |
| `lib/providers/` | Riverpod state. Call services, then update state. | Build widgets |
| `lib/services/` | Saving, syncing, and running the timer. | Import Flutter widgets |
| `lib/models/` | Data shapes with `toJson` / `fromJson`. | I/O or UI |

Screens never call the server. `accountProvider` is the only path that syncs.

The website calls the server only through `website/js/api.js`. New server URLs go in `server/lib/` and get a test in `server/test/`.

## Where the edit goes

Put the edit in the file that already owns that job. Split only when one file is doing two jobs: one screen per file, shared pieces in `lib/widgets/`. A long plan list, skin table, or test can stay in one file. Do not start a second copy of the same idea.

## Rules that do not apply here

The personal "Best Coding Practices" user rule does not apply in this repo. Skip its JavaScript and Python limits, the 50-line function cap, the 500-line file cap, a unit test for every public function, and required comments or try/catch on every call. This file and the other three project rules are how StackFit is written.

## What the analyzer does not catch

- Files are `snake_case`, types are `PascalCase`, providers end in `Provider`.
- Await a future you start, or mark it unawaited on purpose.
- An empty `catch` is only for a deliberate fallback (bad JSON already on disk), and a one-line comment says so.
- New merge, sync, or plan-library behavior gets a test in `test/` or `server/test/`.

```dart
// ❌ BAD — a screen talking to the server
await http.post(AppConstants.accountServerUrl);

// ✅ GOOD — the screen asks the provider
await ref.read(accountProvider.notifier).syncIfSignedIn();
```
